  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1144 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nToday - Sunday: Light Precipitation Chances\n\nShortwave ridging builds into the region today that leads to mild \nand dry conditions with temperatures getting into the low to mid \n40s. Then heading into tonight a trough moves down into the Upper \nMidwest bringing a weak front with it. The best lift with this \nsystem stays to the north of the forecast area leaving the question \nas whether or not we see any precipitation in our area. Forecast \nsoundings show saturation through most of the lowest 20kft or so, \nhowever each of them show a dry sub-cloud layer in the lowest few \nthousand feet that will need to be overcome in order for the \nprecipitation to reach the ground. This supports the lower \nconfidence precipitation forecast for tonight. \n\nThe other question for the forecast for tonight is precipitation \ntype. Surface temperatures will be within 5 degrees plus or minus of \n32F. Most locations south of I-94 will stay as rain with \ntemperatures remaining above freezing while areas north of I-94 may \nsee rain/snow mixing. The latest CAM guidance continues to show a \nsmall area of freezing rain for areas along and north of I-94. There \nare some caveats to this though. The first being that the lowest few \nthousand feet has a dry sub-cloud layer that would need to be \novercome in order for any precipitation to reach the ground. \nSecondly, while forecast soundings show that there is some lift \nwhere precipitation is expected to fall, between 5000 and 10000ft, \nthey do not show that same lift for the lowest 5000ft and this is \nsupported by how weak the overall lift is. The better potential \nwould be for areas in Taylor and Clark counties for any wintry mix \nto occur (20 to 40%). While freezing rain is possible, there are too \nmany uncertainties to confidently say that it will occur. We shall \nsee if the morning suite of models can hone in on a solution and \nprovide more confidence in whether or not freezing rain will occur \nor if any precipitation for that matter will occur.  \n\nAfter the front moves through during the morning, the region remains \nin west to southwest flow which helps warm up temperatures into the \nmid 40s to low 50s. Heading into Saturday night, the models are \ncontinuing to show some phasing between the northern and southern \nstreams. While this is the case, there is concern that with the \nstronger system being in the southern stream that the precipitation \nmight be too far north. Over the past couple of model runs, the \nsouthern stream shortwave is strengthening and digging further and \nfurther south. This trend has been continually supported by both AI \nand ML ensemble probabilities for measurable precipitation which \nkeeps the highest percentages in central and eastern Iowa. Something \nof not through, is that wherever this precipitation ends up, \nboundary layer temperatures might be cool enough on the northern \nedge for some light snow.\n\nNext Week - Seasonal and Dry\n\nAfter the weekend, zonal upper level flow is expected to prevail \nover the region through midweek. This pattern is not favorable for \nprecipitation so dry conditions are currently anticipated beyond \nSunday into midweek. The next chance for widespread precipitation \n(15 to 25%) is Thursday as an upper level low moves into the region \nfrom the central Plains. With zonal flow overhead, temperatures \nshould stay above normal with highs in the 40s and potential some \n50s by midweek.",
